title: Tag entries and references
tags: [[Hierarchical tags]]
kind: reference
aliases: tag-entry
summary: What a tag entry holds, how records point at one, and the [tag] table that names every field.

!! A tag entry

|!Field |!Type |!Meaning |
|`mf_schema` |`string` = `tag` |marks the metarecord as a tag entry |
|`path` |`hierarchy_ref`, one row per parent (`tree_ref` in a repository created before October 2026) |its positions: parent entry and name, the same name on every row ([[Hierarchy references]]). A top-level tag is a root, so paths have no leading `/` ([[TreeRef path conventions]]) |
|`label` |`string` |optional display name |
|`always`, `partition`, `sparse` |`bool` |how the tag is asked about and what it excludes — absent is false ([[Tag flags]]) |

An entry with no resolvable `path` is not part of the vocabulary. `mf tag` creates
entries as it needs them — a missing `cinema/thriller` is created with the missing
`cinema` above it — with `mf_schema` and `path` only; the shipped schema declares the
three flags with the default `false`, so an entry made in the GUI shows them. The
`exclusive` flag of older vocabularies has no effect ([[Why there is no exclusive flag]]).

!! On a record

|!Field |!Type |!Meaning |
|`tag` |`ref`, repeatable |the record has these tags |
|`negative_tag` |`ref`, repeatable |the record has not these tags |
|`mixed_tag` |`ref`, repeatable |a folder partly tagged: decided child by child |

The shipped schema declares `tag` on `note` and `bookmark` ([[Schema file]]).

!! Field names

Every name above except `label` comes from the `[tag]` table of the CLI configuration
(`type-field`, `entry-type`, `path-field`, `positive`, `negative`, `mixed`, `always`,
`partition`, `sparse`); the
defaults are the names shown ([[CLI configuration]]).
The shipped scripts, the simplified grammar's tag forms and the GUI's ref pickers use
the defaults.
